        "summary": "Build professional, scalable RESTful APIs with Express.js. Master the middleware execution chain, sub-routers, request parameter handling, JSON parsing, schema validation, and standardized error responses.",
        "content": "### Learning Objectives\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n1. Initialize an Express application with standard middleware (`express.json()`, `express.urlencoded()`, `cors`, `morgan`).\n2. Design clean, predictable RESTful API endpoints adhering to standard HTTP verbs and URI resource naming conventions.\n3. Understand the Express Middleware Pipeline: `req`, `res`, `next()`, and error-handling middleware signatures.\n4. Separate route definitions, controller handlers, and business logic for high maintainability.\n5. Validate incoming request headers, URL parameters, query strings, and body payloads robustly.\n\n---\n\n### 1. RESTful API Design Principles\n\n**REST** (Representational State Transfer) organizes network services around **resources** identified by clean URIs:\n\n- **Nouns over verbs**: Use `/api/courses` instead of `/api/getAllCourses` or `/api/deleteCourse`.\n- **Plural resource collections**: `/api/courses`, `/api/users`, `/api/enrollments`.\n- **Sub-resources for relationships**: `/api/courses/be-1/lessons`, `/api/users/42/orders`.\n- **Use standard HTTP Methods**:\n\n| Method | URI Path | Purpose | Success Status |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`GET`** | `/api/courses` | Fetch a list of courses (supports `?category=tech&page=1`) | `200 OK` |\n| **`GET`** | `/api/courses/:id` | Fetch a single course by ID or alias | `200 OK` |\n| **`POST`** | `/api/courses` | Create a new course record | `201 Created` |\n| **`PUT`** | `/api/courses/:id` | Replace an entire course resource | `200 OK` |\n| **`PATCH`** | `/api/courses/:id` | Update specific fields of a course (e.g. price) | `200 OK` |\n| **`DELETE`** | `/api/courses/:id` | Remove a course resource | `204 No Content` / `200 OK` |\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Express Middleware Pipeline\n\nIn Express, **middleware** functions are functions that have access to the Request object (`req`), Response object (`res`), and the next middleware function (`next`) in the application’s request-response cycle.\n\n```\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                     EXPRESS MIDDLEWARE EXECUTION CHAIN                   |\n|                                                                          |\n|  Incoming HTTP Request                                                   |\n|        |                                                                 |\n|        v                                                                 |\n|  [ 1. Logging Middleware (morgan / custom timestamp logger) ]            |\n|        | ---> next()                                                     |\n|        v                                                                 |\n|  [ 2. Body Parser (express.json()) ]                                     |\n|        | ---> next()                                                     |\n|        v                                                                 |\n|  [ 3. Authentication (Verify JWT Token -> attach req.user) ]             |\n|        | ---> next() (or res.status(401).json() if invalid)              |\n|        v                                                                 |\n|  [ 4. Validation Middleware (Check required fields & types) ]            |\n|        | ---> next()                                                     |\n|        v                                                                 |\n|  [ 5. Controller Route Handler (Business Logic & DB Query) ]             |\n|        | ---> res.status(200).json(data)                                 |\n|        v                                                                 |\n|  (If error thrown: skips directly to Global Error Handler)               |\n|        v                                                                 |\n|  [ 6. Global Error Handler (err, req, res, next) ]                       |\n|        | ---> res.status(err.statusCode || 500).json({ error: ... })     |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Complete Express Architecture Example\n\n#### A. Controller Handler (`controllers/courseController.js`)\n```javascript\nconst coursesData = require('../data/coursesData');\n\nconst getCourseById = async (req, res, next) => {\n  try {\n    const { id } = req.params;\n    const course = coursesData.find(c => c.id === id || (c.aliases && c.aliases.includes(id)));\n\n    if (!course) {\n      return res.status(404).json({\n        success: false,\n        error: `Course with ID '${id}' not found`\n      });\n    }\n\n    res.status(200).json({\n      success: true,\n      course\n    });\n  } catch (err) {\n    next(err); // Forwards to global error handler\n  }\n};\n\nmodule.exports = { getCourseById };\n```\n\n#### B. Router (`routes/courseRoutes.js`)\n```javascript\nconst express = require('express');\nconst router = express.Router();\nconst { getCourseById } = require('../controllers/courseController');\n\nrouter.get('/:id', getCourseById);\n\nmodule.exports = router;\n```\n\n#### C. Application Entrypoint (`app.js`)\n```javascript\nconst express = require('express');\nconst cors = require('cors');\nconst courseRoutes = require('./routes/courseRoutes');\n\nconst app = express();\n\n// Standard middleware\napp.use(cors());\napp.use(express.json());\n\n// Mount routers\napp.use('/api/courses', courseRoutes);\n\n// 404 Catch-all handler\napp.use((req, res, next) => {\n  res.status(404).json({ success: false, error: 'Endpoint not found' });\n});\n\n// Global Centralized Error Handler (4 arguments are mandatory!)\napp.use((err, req, res, next) => {\n  console.error('[SERVER ERROR]', err.stack);\n  const status = err.statusCode || 500;\n  res.status(status).json({\n    success: false,\n    error: err.message || 'Internal Server Error'\n  });\n});\n\nmodule.exports = app;\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Practical Request Validation Middleware\n\nNever trust client payloads. Validate types, constraints, and presence before invoking business logic:\n\n```javascript\n// middleware/validateEnrollment.js\nconst validateEnrollment = (req, res, next) => {\n  const { courseId, phoneNumber, paymentMethod } = req.body;\n\n  if (!courseId || typeof courseId !== 'string') {\n    return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Field \"courseId\" is required and must be a string.' });\n  }\n\n  if (paymentMethod === 'mpesa') {\n    const phoneRegex = /^(?:254|+254|0)?(7(?:(?:[0-9][0-9])|(?:0[0-8]))[0-9]{6})$/;\n    if (!phoneNumber || !phoneRegex.test(phoneNumber.trim())) {\n      return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Valid Kenyan Safaricom phone number required for M-Pesa STK push.' });\n    }\n  }\n\n  next();\n};\n\nmodule.exports = validateEnrollment;\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. Module Summary & Key Takeaways\n- REST APIs organize resources under clean plural noun URIs with standard HTTP verbs.\n- Express middleware runs sequentially using `next()`.\n- Centralize all error handling in a 4-argument Express error middleware (`(err, req, res, next)`).\n- Always validate incoming request payloads before passing them to services or databases.",
