        "number": 6,
        "title": "React From Zero",
        "subtitle": "Declarative UI, JSX, component composition, useState, useEffect, custom hooks, and state architecture",
        "readingTime": "32 min",
        "videoLesson": {
          "title": "React JS Crash Course",
          "channel": "Traversy Media",
          "duration": "01:48:47",
          "videoId": "w7ejDZ8SWv8",
          "watchUrl": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=w7ejDZ8SWv8",
          "embedUrl": "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/w7ejDZ8SWv8",
          "url": "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/w7ejDZ8SWv8",
          "alternativeSource": "https://react.dev",
          "sourceName": "React.dev — Official Documentation",
          "badge": "Verified Reference Video"
        },
        "summary": "Master the industry-standard UI library. Understand declarative component architecture, dynamic JSX, state management with hooks, side effects, context, and custom reusable hooks.",
        "content": "### Learning Objectives\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n1. Explain the declarative UI paradigm and Virtual DOM reconciliation.\n2. Author reusable functional components using JSX syntax, props, and conditional rendering.\n3. Manage component state immutably using the `useState` hook.\n4. Execute side effects, API requests, and subscription cleanups using the `useEffect` hook.\n5. Create custom reusable hooks to encapsulate complex business and UI logic.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Declarative Paradigm & Virtual DOM\n\nIn traditional JavaScript (imperative), you tell the browser **how** to change every DOM node step-by-step. In React (declarative), you describe **what** the UI should look like for a given state:\n\n```\nState Changes  --->  React generates new Virtual DOM  --->  Diffs with previous V-DOM  --->  Minimal Real DOM Updates\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Components, Props & JSX\n\n```jsx\n// 1. Reusable Course Card Component\nfunction CourseCard({ title, price, category, onEnroll }) {\n  return (\n    <div className=\"p-6 rounded-2xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 hover:border-blue-500 transition-all\">\n      <span className=\"text-xs font-bold text-blue-400 uppercase tracking-widest\">{category}</span>\n      <h3 className=\"text-xl font-bold text-white mt-1\">{title}</h3>\n      <p className=\"text-sm text-slate-400 mt-2\">Tuition: KSh {price.toLocaleString()} / mo</p>\n      \n      <button \n        onClick={onEnroll}\n        className=\"mt-4 w-full py-2.5 px-4 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-500 text-white font-bold rounded-xl transition-colors\"\n      >\n        Enroll in Course\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. State Management with `useState`\n\n```jsx\nimport React, { useState } from 'react';\n\nfunction Counter() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n\n  const increment = () => setCount(prev => prev + 1);\n  const decrement = () => setCount(prev => Math.max(0, prev - 1));\n\n  return (\n    <div className=\"flex items-center gap-4\">\n      <button onClick={decrement} className=\"px-3 py-1 bg-slate-800 rounded text-white\">-</button>\n      <span className=\"text-lg font-bold text-white\">{count}</span>\n      <button onClick={increment} className=\"px-3 py-1 bg-slate-800 rounded text-white\">+</button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Side Effects & Data Fetching with `useEffect`\n\n```jsx\nimport React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\n\nfunction CourseCatalog() {\n  const [courses, setCourses] = useState([]);\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    let isMounted = true;\n\n    async function fetchCourses() {\n      try {\n        const res = await fetch('/api/books');\n        const data = await res.json();\n        if (isMounted) {\n          setCourses(data.books || []);\n        }\n      } catch (err) {\n        console.error('Failed to load courses:', err);\n      } finally {\n        if (isMounted) setLoading(false);\n      }\n    }\n\n    fetchCourses();\n\n    return () => {\n      isMounted = false; // Cleanup on unmount\n    };\n  }, []); // Empty array: runs once when component mounts\n\n  if (loading) return <div className=\"text-white\">Loading FAHMRISE catalog...</div>;\n\n  return (\n    <div className=\"grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6\">\n      {courses.map(course => (\n        <CourseCard key={course.id} {...course} onEnroll={() => alert(`Enrolled in ${course.title}`)} />\n      ))}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. Activities & Practical Exercises\n\n#### Exercise 1: Controlled Search Filter Component\nBuild a React component with an input search box and a list of items. As the user types into the input, update local state using `useState`, filter the items array dynamically, and render matching items with a fallback message when no results match.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Module Knowledge Quiz\n\n**Question 1:** Why must state in React never be mutated directly (e.g. `state.count = 5`)?\n- A) It causes a syntax error in the browser\n- B) React relies on object reference equality to detect changes and trigger UI re-renders *(Correct)*\n- C) It deletes all local storage data\n- D) It converts components into class components\n*Explanation: Direct state mutations bypass React's reconciliation engine, causing the component to fail to re-render.*\n\n**Question 2:** What does an empty dependency array `[]` passed to `useEffect` mean?\n- A) The effect runs on every single re-render\n- B) The effect runs only once when the component mounts *(Correct)*\n- C) The effect is permanently disabled\n- D) The effect runs only on component unmount\n*Explanation: An empty dependency array tells React that the effect does not depend on any props or state, so it runs once after initial mount.*\n\n---\n\n### 7. Module Summary & Key Takeaways\n- React components are pure functions of props and state that return JSX.\n- Manage local dynamic values with `useState` and always update state immutably.\n- Execute network requests, subscriptions, and DOM side effects inside `useEffect`.\n- Pass data down through props and lift state up to common ancestors when sharing data across components.",
        "challenge": {
          "type": "quiz",
          "title": "Module 6 Checkpoint: React Architecture",
          "question": "When rendering a dynamic list of elements in React with .map(), why is providing a unique 'key' prop mandatory?",
          "options": [
            "To style the elements with CSS automatically",
            "To help React identify which items have changed, been added, or removed during Virtual DOM diffing",
            "To convert the list into an HTML table",
            "To enable double-click event listeners"
          ],
          "correctAnswer": 1,
          "explanation": "Unique keys give elements a stable identity across renders, allowing React to update only the specific DOM nodes that changed."
        }
