        "title": "TypeScript and Modern Frontend Tools",
        "subtitle": "Static typing, interfaces, generics, Vite tooling, ESLint, and Tailwind CSS",
        "readingTime": "26 min",
        "videoLesson": {
          "title": "TypeScript Tutorial for Beginners",
          "channel": "Programming with Mosh",
          "duration": "01:04:28",
          "videoId": "d56mG7DezGs",
          "watchUrl": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d56mG7DezGs",
          "embedUrl": "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/d56mG7DezGs",
          "url": "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/d56mG7DezGs",
          "alternativeSource": "https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/",
          "sourceName": "TypeScript Official Handbook & Documentation",
          "badge": "Verified Reference Video"
        },
        "summary": "Upgrade your frontend engineering with TypeScript. Prevent runtime bugs, build type-safe React components, author interfaces and generics, and leverage high-speed Vite tooling.",
        "content": "### Learning Objectives\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n1. Explain how static type checking eliminates runtime exceptions before code reaches production.\n2. Define primitive types, union types, interfaces, and type aliases for complex data models.\n3. Strongly type React functional components, props, event handlers, and custom hooks.\n4. Author reusable generic functions and type definitions (`<T>`).\n5. Configure modern developer tooling: Vite, ESLint, Prettier, and Tailwind CSS.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Why TypeScript? JavaScript with Superpowers\n\nJavaScript is dynamically typed, meaning variable types are only evaluated at runtime when code executes in the user's browser. TypeScript adds **static type checking**, catching errors during compilation in your editor.\n\n```typescript\n// Pure TypeScript Interface\ninterface LearnerProfile {\n  id: string;\n  fullName: string;\n  email: string;\n  enrolledCourseIds: string[];\n  subscriptionActive: boolean;\n  role: 'learner' | 'instructor' | 'admin';\n  avatarUrl?: string; // Optional field\n}\n\n// Function with explicit parameter and return types\nfunction canAccessCourse(user: LearnerProfile, targetCourseId: string): boolean {\n  if (user.role === 'admin') return true;\n  return user.subscriptionActive || user.enrolledCourseIds.includes(targetCourseId);\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Typing React Components with TypeScript\n\n```tsx\nimport React, { useState } from 'react';\n\ninterface ButtonProps {\n  label: string;\n  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary' | 'danger';\n  disabled?: boolean;\n  onClick: (event: React.MouseEvent<HTMLButtonElement>) => void;\n  children?: React.ReactNode;\n}\n\nexport const ActionButton: React.FC<ButtonProps> = ({\n  label,\n  variant = 'primary',\n  disabled = false,\n  onClick,\n  children\n}) => {\n  const baseClasses = \"px-4 py-2 rounded-xl font-bold transition-all\";\n  const variantClasses = variant === 'primary' \n    ? \"bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-500 text-white\" \n    : \"bg-slate-800 hover:bg-slate-700 text-slate-200\";\n\n  return (\n    <button\n      disabled={disabled}\n      onClick={onClick}\n      className={`${baseClasses} ${variantClasses} ${disabled ? 'opacity-50 cursor-not-allowed' : ''}`}\n    >\n      {label}\n      {children}\n    </button>\n  );\n};\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Generics in TypeScript\n\nGenerics allow writing flexible, reusable code that works with any data type while preserving type safety:\n\n```typescript\n// Generic API Response Wrapper\ninterface ApiResponse<T> {\n  success: boolean;\n  data: T;\n  message?: string;\n  timestamp: string;\n}\n\n// Generic Fetch Helper\nasync function apiGet<T>(endpoint: string): Promise<ApiResponse<T>> {\n  const response = await fetch(endpoint);\n  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Network error: ${response.status}`);\n  return response.json();\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Modern Tooling: Vite & Tailwind CSS\n\n- **Vite**: Ultra-fast next-generation frontend build tool powered by native ES Modules (ESM) and esbuild. Delivers instantaneous Hot Module Replacement (HMR).\n- **Tailwind CSS**: Utility-first CSS framework that speeds up UI construction with composable classes (`flex`, `items-center`, `p-4`, `bg-slate-900`).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Activities & Practical Exercises\n\n#### Exercise 1: Strongly Typed Course Filter\nDefine a TypeScript `interface Course` with fields `id`, `title`, `price`, `category`, and `published`. Write a typed function `filterCoursesByCategory(courses: Course[], category: string): Course[]` and verify with the TypeScript compiler that invalid property accesses produce compile-time errors.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Module Knowledge Quiz\n\n**Question 1:** What is the key advantage of using TypeScript over plain JavaScript in large applications?\n- A) It removes the need to write HTML\n- B) It catches type mismatches, typos, and undefined property errors at compile-time before code runs *(Correct)*\n- C) It eliminates the need for unit testing\n- D) It automatically deploys code to the cloud\n*Explanation: TypeScript's type checker identifies errors during authoring and compilation, drastically reducing runtime production bugs.*\n\n**Question 2:** How do you define an optional property in a TypeScript interface?\n- A) `fieldName: optional string;`\n- B) `fieldName?: string;` *(Correct)*\n- C) `fieldName: string | null;`\n- D) `@optional fieldName: string;`\n*Explanation: Appending a question mark (?) to a property name in TypeScript marks it as optional.*\n\n---\n\n### 7. Module Summary & Key Takeaways\n- TypeScript introduces static type checking on top of modern JavaScript.\n- Use interfaces and type aliases to establish unambiguous data contracts.\n- Strongly type React components, props, event handlers, and generic API calls.\n- Vite and Tailwind CSS provide a blazing fast development experience with instant feedback.",
