        "summary": "Build bulletproof, inclusive, high-speed web apps. Master unit testing with Vitest and React Testing Library, WCAG accessibility compliance, screen reader compatibility, and Core Web Vitals optimization.",
        "content": "### Learning Objectives\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n1. Write automated unit and component tests using Vitest and React Testing Library.\n2. Test user interactions from the user's perspective without testing implementation details.\n3. Audit and fix accessibility barriers to meet **WCAG 2.2 Level AA** compliance.\n4. Measure and optimize Google's **Core Web Vitals**: Largest Contentful Paint (LCP), Interaction to Next Paint (INP), and Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS).\n5. Implement asset optimization, lazy loading, code splitting, and memoization.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Component Testing with React Testing Library & Vitest\n\nThe guiding principle of React Testing Library: **\"The more your tests resemble the way your software is used, the more confidence they can give you.\"**\n\n```tsx\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react';\nimport userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';\nimport { describe, it, expect, vi } from 'vitest';\nimport '@testing-library/jest-dom';\nimport { CourseCard } from './CourseCard';\n\ndescribe('CourseCard Component', () => {\n  it('renders course title and handles enrollment click', async () => {\n    const user = userEvent.setup();\n    const handleEnroll = vi.fn();\n\n    render(\n      <CourseCard \n        title=\"Frontend Developer From Zero to Job-Ready\" \n        price={20000} \n        category=\"Professional Development Pathways\" \n        onEnroll={handleEnroll} \n      />\n    );\n\n    // 1. Query by Accessible Role and Text\n    expect(screen.getByRole('heading', { level: 3 })).toHaveTextContent(/Frontend Developer/i);\n    expect(screen.getByText(/KSh 20,000/i)).toBeInTheDocument();\n\n    // 2. Simulate User Click\n    const enrollButton = screen.getByRole('button', { name: /enroll in course/i });\n    await user.click(enrollButton);\n\n    // 3. Assert callback was called\n    expect(handleEnroll).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);\n  });\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Web Accessibility (WCAG 2.2 Standards)\n\nAccessibility ensures everyone—including people with visual, auditory, motor, or cognitive impairments—can use your web application.\n\n```\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                       THE 4 PRINCIPLES OF ACCESSIBILITY (POUR)           |\n|                                                                          |\n| 1. PERCEIVABLE: Content must be presentable to users via multiple senses |\n|    (e.g., text alternatives for images, high color contrast >= 4.5:1)   |\n|                                                                          |\n| 2. OPERABLE: UI components and navigation must be operable via keyboard  |\n|    (e.g., full keyboard tab navigation, visible focus outlines)          |\n|                                                                          |\n| 3. UNDERSTANDABLE: Information and operation must be clear and intuitive |\n|    (e.g., helpful form error messages, predictable navigation)           |\n|                                                                          |\n| 4. ROBUST: Content must be interpretable by diverse user agents/devices  |\n|    (e.g., valid semantic HTML, standard ARIA roles when needed)         |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Core Web Vitals & Frontend Performance Optimization\n\nGoogle evaluates website user experience using three primary metrics:\n\n1. **LCP (Largest Contentful Paint)**: Measures loading performance. Target: **<= 2.5 seconds**.\n   - *Fix*: Optimize hero images (WebP/AVIF), preload critical assets, eliminate render-blocking CSS.\n2. **INP (Interaction to Next Paint)**: Measures user responsiveness and input delay. Target: **<= 200 ms**.\n   - *Fix*: Break long JavaScript tasks into smaller chunks, optimize event handlers, avoid synchronous heavy loops.\n3. **CLS (Cumulative Layout Shift)**: Measures visual stability. Target: **<= 0.1**.\n   - *Fix*: Always declare explicit `width` and `height` on images and reserve space for dynamic banners.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Code Splitting & Dynamic Imports\n\n```jsx\nimport React, { Suspense, lazy } from 'react';\n\n// Lazy load heavy component only when needed\nconst HeavyCodeEditor = lazy(() => import('./HeavyCodeEditor'));\n\nexport function LessonWorkspace() {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>Interactive Coding Workspace</h1>\n      <Suspense fallback={<div className=\"p-4 text-white\">Loading Editor...</div>}>\n        <HeavyCodeEditor />\n      </Suspense>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. Activities & Practical Exercises\n\n#### Exercise 1: Accessibility & Lighthouse Audit\nOpen Chrome DevTools on your portfolio or project page. Open the **Lighthouse** tab, run a full **Accessibility** and **Performance** audit, identify any color contrast or missing label warnings, and remediate them until you achieve a score of 95+.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Module Knowledge Quiz\n\n**Question 1:** When writing automated component tests with React Testing Library, which query method is most recommended for accessibility?\n- A) `container.querySelector('.btn-blue-submit')`\n- B) `screen.getByRole('button', { name: /submit/i })` *(Correct)*\n- C) `document.getElementsByTagName('button')[0]`\n- D) `screen.getByTestId('test-submit-button')`\n*Explanation: Querying by role and accessible name mirrors how users and assistive screen readers find and interact with elements.*\n\n**Question 2:** What is the primary cause of Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) on a webpage?\n- A) Using dark mode styling\n- B) Images or ad banners loading without predefined width and height dimensions, causing content to jump unexpectedly *(Correct)*\n- C) Having more than three JavaScript functions\n- D) Using semantic HTML tags\n*Explanation: When images or embeds load without dimensions, the browser shifts surrounding text down suddenly, creating jarring layout shifts.*\n\n---\n\n### 7. Module Summary & Key Takeaways\n- Test component behavior from the user's perspective with Vitest and React Testing Library.\n- Comply with WCAG 2.2 standards: accessible keyboard navigation, color contrast, and semantic labels.\n- Optimize Core Web Vitals (LCP < 2.5s, INP < 200ms, CLS < 0.1) for superior UX and SEO.\n- Utilize code splitting and lazy loading to keep initial JavaScript bundles lean.",
