        "number": 7,
        "title": "Backend Security",
        "subtitle": "Input validation, CORS, Helmet security headers, rate limiting, preventing OWASP Top 10 vulnerabilities, and safe error handling",
        "readingTime": "22 min",
        "videoLesson": {
          "title": "Backend Security, Rate Limiting & OWASP Top 10 Protections",
          "channel": "freeCodeCamp.org",
          "duration": "02:40:00",
          "videoId": "ug8W0sFiVJo",
          "watchUrl": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ug8W0sFiVJo",
          "embedUrl": "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/ug8W0sFiVJo",
          "url": "https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/ug8W0sFiVJo",
          "alternativeSource": "https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/",
          "sourceName": "OWASP Top 10 API Security Risks",
          "badge": "Verified Reference Video"
        },
        "summary": "Harden your backend applications against real-world cyber attacks. Protect your servers against SQL Injection, Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) misconfigurations, brute-force attacks with rate limiting, and sensitive data leakage.",
        "content": "### Learning Objectives\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n1. Identify and mitigate the OWASP Top 10 API Security vulnerabilities in Node.js applications.\n2. Configure **CORS** (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) securely without using wildcards (`*`) in production.\n3. Apply HTTP security headers with **Helmet** (Content Security Policy, X-Frame-Options, HSTS).\n4. Implement IP-based and user-based **Rate Limiting** to prevent brute-force attacks and DDoS abuse.\n5. Sanitize user inputs and eliminate SQL injection, NoSQL injection, and Path Traversal risks.\n6. Prevent information leakage in production error messages and stack traces.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Defending Against the OWASP Top 10 API Risks\n\nThe Open Worldwide Application Security Project (OWASP) identifies critical security risks:\n\n```\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                    OWASP TOP BACKEND RISKS & MITIGATIONS                 |\n|                                                                          |\n|  1. Broken Object Level Auth (BOLA / IDOR)                               |\n|     - Attack: GET /api/users/99/orders by User 42                        |\n|     - Defense: Always check req.user.id === resource.ownerId             |\n|                                                                          |\n|  2. SQL Injection                                                        |\n|     - Attack: SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '' OR '1'='1';           |\n|     - Defense: Always use parameterized queries ($1, $2)                 |\n|                                                                          |\n|  3. Brute Force & Rate Limit Abuse                                       |\n|     - Attack: 10,000 password guesses per minute on /api/auth/login      |\n|     - Defense: Rate limit with express-rate-limit (e.g. 5 attempts/15m)  |\n|                                                                          |\n|  4. Mass Assignment Vulnerability                                        |\n|     - Attack: Sending { \"role\": \"admin\" } in registration payload        |\n|     - Defense: Whitelist allowed fields explicitly in controllers        |\n|                                                                          |\n|  5. Sensitive Data Exposure                                              |\n|     - Attack: Returning password hashes or raw DB error stack traces     |\n|     - Defense: Strip sensitive fields before returning JSON              |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Securing HTTP Headers with Helmet\n\n`helmet` sets 15+ standard HTTP security headers that protect against clickjacking, MIME sniffing, and cross-site scripting:\n\n```javascript\nconst express = require('express');\nconst helmet = require('helmet');\nconst app = express();\n\n// Enable secure HTTP headers\napp.use(helmet());\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Strict CORS Configuration\n\nNever use `app.use(cors())` with open wildcard access (`*`) on authenticated banking or educational APIs in production:\n\n```javascript\nconst cors = require('cors');\n\nconst allowedOrigins = [\n  'https://fahmrise.com',\n  'https://app.fahmrise.com',\n  'http://localhost:5173' // Local dev frontend\n];\n\nconst corsOptions = {\n  origin: (origin, callback) => {\n    // Allow requests with no origin (e.g. mobile apps, curl) or allowed origins\n    if (!origin || allowedOrigins.includes(origin)) {\n      callback(null, true);\n    } else {\n      callback(new Error('Blocked by CORS policy: Origin not allowed'));\n    }\n  },\n  credentials: true,\n  methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'PATCH', 'DELETE'],\n  allowedHeaders: ['Content-Type', 'Authorization']\n};\n\napp.use(cors(corsOptions));\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Rate Limiting to Stop Brute Force Attacks\n\nProtect sensitive endpoints (login, password reset, payment verification) using `express-rate-limit`:\n\n```javascript\nconst rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');\n\n// Strict limiter for authentication routes\nconst authLimiter = rateLimit({\n  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, // 15 minutes window\n  max: 5, // Limit each IP to 5 login attempts per window\n  message: {\n    success: false,\n    error: 'Too many login attempts from this IP address. Please try again after 15 minutes.'\n  },\n  standardHeaders: true,\n  legacyHeaders: false\n});\n\n// General API limiter\nconst apiLimiter = rateLimit({\n  windowMs: 60 * 1000, // 1 minute\n  max: 120, // 120 requests per minute\n  standardHeaders: true\n});\n\n// Apply to routes\napp.use('/api/', apiLimiter);\napp.use('/api/auth/login', authLimiter);\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. Safe Error Handling Without Information Leakage\n\nIn production, never send raw database error messages or full stack traces to the client:\n\n```javascript\n// middleware/errorHandler.js\nconst errorHandler = (err, req, res, next) => {\n  const isProduction = process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production';\n  const statusCode = err.statusCode || 500;\n\n  // Log full error details internally for developers/monitoring\n  console.error(`[${new Date().toISOString()}] ${req.method} ${req.url} - Error:`, err);\n\n  res.status(statusCode).json({\n    success: false,\n    error: isProduction && statusCode === 500 \n      ? 'An unexpected server error occurred. Please contact support.' \n      : err.message,\n    ...(isProduction ? {} : { stack: err.stack })\n  });\n};\n```\n\n---\n\n### 6. Module Summary & Key Takeaways\n- Apply the Principle of Least Privilege across database accounts and API endpoints.\n- Secure HTTP headers with `helmet` and restrict allowed domains with `cors`.\n- Apply strict rate limiters on authentication and payment endpoints to block brute-force attacks.\n- Never expose internal database stack traces to clients in production.",
