        "summary": "Combine normalized relational database tables with precision. Master Venn diagram join semantics, handle mismatched keys, prevent Cartesian product explosions, and query hierarchical self-joins.",
        "content": "### Learning Objectives\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n1. Explain how relational databases normalize data into separate tables to eliminate redundancy.\n2. Master the syntax and behavior of **`INNER JOIN`** (only matching keys from both tables).\n3. Master **`LEFT JOIN` (LEFT OUTER JOIN)** (all rows from left table, matching or NULLs from right table).\n4. Identify orphaned records and non-converting leads using `LEFT JOIN ... WHERE right_table.id IS NULL`.\n5. Understand **`FULL OUTER JOIN`**, **`CROSS JOIN`** (Cartesian products), and **`Self-Joins`** (hierarchical manager-employee relationships).\n\n---\n\n### 1. The 4 Fundamental SQL JOIN Types\n\n```\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                             SQL JOIN CHEAT SHEET                              |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| JOIN Type        | Rows Returned                                              |\n+------------------+------------------------------------------------------------+\n| INNER JOIN       | Only rows where the join key matches in BOTH tables        |\n| LEFT JOIN        | ALL rows from Table A, plus matching rows from Table B     |\n| RIGHT JOIN       | ALL rows from Table B, plus matching rows from Table A     |\n| FULL OUTER JOIN  | ALL rows from BOTH tables (filling missing sides with NULL)|\n| CROSS JOIN       | Cartesian product: Every row in A combined with all in B   |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Practical Examples: Kenya Retail & M-Pesa Ecosystem\n\n#### A. INNER JOIN: Combining Orders, Customers, and Products\n```sql\nSELECT \n    o.order_id,\n    o.order_date,\n    c.full_name AS customer_name,\n    c.city,\n    p.product_name,\n    p.category,\n    o.quantity,\n    (o.quantity * p.unit_price_ksh) AS order_value_ksh\nFROM orders AS o\nINNER JOIN customers AS c ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id\nINNER JOIN products  AS p ON o.product_id  = p.product_id\nWHERE o.status = 'Delivered';\n```\n\n#### B. LEFT JOIN: Finding Inactive / Dormant Customers (Anti-Join Pattern)\nWhich registered customers have **never placed an order**?\n\n```sql\nSELECT \n    c.customer_id,\n    c.full_name,\n    c.phone_number,\n    c.created_at AS registration_date\nFROM customers AS c\nLEFT JOIN orders AS o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.order_id IS NULL; -- The Anti-Join filter!\n```\n\n#### C. Self-Join: Employee & Manager Hierarchy\n```sql\nSELECT \n    e.employee_id,\n    e.full_name AS staff_name,\n    e.job_title,\n    m.full_name AS direct_manager_name\nFROM employees AS e\nLEFT JOIN employees AS m ON e.manager_id = m.employee_id;\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Summary & Best Practices\n- Always alias your tables (`FROM orders AS o`) for readability and brevity.\n- Explicitly state join conditions with `ON a.id = b.id` to avoid accidental Cartesian products.",
