        "summary": "Understand the high-performance mathematical foundation of Python data science: NumPy. Master the ndarray, vectorized element-wise arithmetic, boolean filtering, array broadcasting, and statistical operations.",
        "content": "### Learning Objectives\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n1. Explain why **NumPy ndarrays** execute 50x–100x faster than native Python lists due to contiguous memory allocation and C-level execution.\n2. Create 1D and 2D arrays using **`np.array()`**, **`np.zeros()`**, **`np.arange()`**, and **`np.linspace()`**.\n3. Perform **Vectorized Element-wise Operations** without writing slow for-loops.\n4. Understand **Array Broadcasting** rules when computing operations between arrays of different dimensions.\n5. Compute statistical metrics: **`np.mean()`**, **`np.median()`**, **`np.std()`**, **`np.percentile()`**, and conditional selection with **`np.where()`**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Power of Vectorization: NumPy vs Python Lists\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Traditional Python List (Slow: Requires for-loop)\nprices_list = [1000, 2500, 4800, 9200]\ndiscounted_list = [p * 0.90 for p in prices_list]\n\n# NumPy Vectorized Array (Fast: Executes in compiled C/Fortran)\nprices_arr = np.array([1000, 2500, 4800, 9200])\ndiscounted_arr = prices_arr * 0.90  # Instant element-wise multiplication!\nprint(discounted_arr)  # array([ 900., 2250., 4320., 8280.])\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Conditional Logic with np.where()\n\n`np.where(condition, value_if_true, value_if_false)` is the NumPy equivalent of Excel's `IF()` function:\n\n```python\ntransaction_amounts = np.array([500, 4500, 12000, 800, 25000, 3200])\n\n# Classify transactions into 'High-Value' vs 'Standard'\ntransaction_tier = np.where(transaction_amounts >= 10000, \"High-Value Tier\", \"Standard Tier\")\nprint(transaction_tier)\n# ['Standard Tier' 'Standard Tier' 'High-Value Tier' 'Standard Tier' 'High-Value Tier' 'Standard Tier']\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Summary & Best Practices\n- Never use Python for-loops to perform mathematical calculations on numeric series; always use vectorized NumPy operations.\n- Use `np.percentile(array, 95)` to establish 95th-percentile SLA benchmarks.",
