        "summary": "Master advanced data wrangling and multi-table integration. Learn the Split-Apply-Combine pattern with groupby/agg, relational dataset joins with pd.merge, vertical stacking with pd.concat, and reshaping with pivot_table and melt.",
        "content": "### Learning Objectives\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n1. Apply the **Split-Apply-Combine** pattern using **`df.groupby()`**.\n2. Compute multiple named aggregations across multiple columns simultaneously using **`.agg()`**.\n3. Merge relational DataFrames using **`pd.merge()`** (inner, left, right, outer joins).\n4. Concatenate multiple DataFrames vertically and horizontally using **`pd.concat()`**.\n5. Reshape datasets between wide and long formats using **`df.pivot_table()`** and **`pd.melt()`**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Named Aggregations with groupby & agg\n\n```python\n# Multi-Metric Branch Performance Summary\nbranch_summary = df.groupby(\"branch_name\").agg(\n    total_revenue_ksh=(\"amount_ksh\", \"sum\"),\n    avg_order_value_ksh=(\"amount_ksh\", \"mean\"),\n    total_orders_count=(\"transaction_id\", \"count\"),\n    unique_buyers=(\"customer_id\", \"nunique\"),\n    max_single_sale=(\"amount_ksh\", \"max\")\n).reset_index()\n\n# Sort by revenue descending\nbranch_summary = branch_summary.sort_values(by=\"total_revenue_ksh\", ascending=False)\nprint(branch_summary)\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Merging Relational DataFrames (SQL-Style Joins)\n\n```python\n# Left Join: Match orders with customer metadata\ndf_merged = pd.merge(\n    df_orders,\n    df_customers,\n    on=\"customer_id\",\n    how=\"left\",\n    suffixes=(\"_order\", \"_customer\")\n)\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Reshaping: pivot_table vs pd.melt\n\n#### A. pivot_table (Long to Wide Summary)\n```python\n# Create a Region by Product Category Revenue Matrix\nrevenue_matrix = df.pivot_table(\n    index=\"region\",\n    columns=\"category\",\n    values=\"amount_ksh\",\n    aggfunc=\"sum\",\n    fill_value=0\n)\n```\n\n#### B. pd.melt (Wide to Long Normalization)\n```python\n# Unpivot monthly columns back to tall normalized format\ndf_tall = pd.melt(\n    df_wide,\n    id_vars=[\"branch_name\", \"product_sku\"],\n    value_vars=[\"Jan_2026\", \"Feb_2026\", \"Mar_2026\"],\n    var_name=\"sales_month\",\n    value_name=\"revenue_ksh\"\n)\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Summary & Best Practices\n- Always call `.reset_index()` after a groupby operation to convert grouping keys back into standard DataFrame columns.\n- Use `pd.merge(..., validate='many_to_one')` to guard against unexpected duplicate join keys.",
