        "summary": "Understand the core architecture of relational database management systems (RDBMS), master table schemas and keys, and learn the critical difference between SQL syntax writing order and database execution order.",
        "content": "### Learning Objectives\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n1. Explain the role of **SQL (Structured Query Language)** and **RDBMS** in modern business analytics.\n2. Differentiate between relational databases (PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite, SQL Server) and document/NoSQL stores.\n3. Understand table anatomy: **Rows (Records/Tuples)**, **Columns (Attributes/Fields)**, **Primary Keys (PK)**, and **Foreign Keys (FK)**.\n4. Memorize and apply the **Logical Order of SQL Query Execution** (FROM -> WHERE -> GROUP BY -> HAVING -> SELECT -> ORDER BY -> LIMIT).\n5. Explain why column aliases defined in SELECT cannot be used in a WHERE clause due to execution order.\n\n---\n\n### 1. What is SQL and Why is It the #1 Skill in Data Analysis?\n\nIn modern companies—such as Safaricom, Equity Bank, or Jumia Kenya—transactional data is stored in **Relational Database Management Systems (RDBMS)**. \n\nSpreadsheets cannot handle 100 million rows or concurrent multi-user ACID transactions. **SQL (Structured Query Language)** is the universal, standardized language used to communicate with, extract from, and analyze data inside relational databases.\n\n```\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                       RELATIONAL TABLE ANATOMY & KEYS                         |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Table: customers                                                              |\n| +-----------------+--------------------+------------------+-----------------+ |\n| | customer_id(PK) | full_name          | city             | tier            | |\n| +-----------------+--------------------+------------------+-----------------+ |\n| | 101             | Wanjiku Mwangi     | Nairobi          | Platinum        | |\n| | 102             | Hassan Ali         | Mombasa          | Gold            | |\n| +-----------------+--------------------+------------------+-----------------+ |\n|        |                                                                      |\n|        +---------------------------+ (1-to-Many Relationship via Foreign Key) |\n|                                    |                                          |\n|                                    v                                          |\n| Table: orders                                                                 |\n| +-----------------+--------------------+------------------+-----------------+ |\n| | order_id (PK)   | customer_id (FK)   | order_date       | total_ksh       | |\n| +-----------------+--------------------+------------------+-----------------+ |\n| | 5001            | 101                | 2026-03-01       | 14,500          | |\n| | 5002            | 101                | 2026-03-12       | 8,200           | |\n| | 5003            | 102                | 2026-03-15       | 32,000          | |\n| +-----------------+--------------------+------------------+-----------------+ |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Golden Secret of SQL: Written Order vs Execution Order\n\nOne of the biggest obstacles for beginners is assuming SQL executes top-to-bottom. It does **not**!\n\n#### Written Syntax Order:\n```sql\n1. SELECT column_name, SUM(amount) AS total_revenue\n2. FROM sales_orders\n3. WHERE status = 'Completed'\n4. GROUP BY column_name\n5. HAVING SUM(amount) > 100000\n6. ORDER BY total_revenue DESC\n7. LIMIT 10;\n```\n\n#### Actual Database Logical Execution Order:\n```\n1. FROM & JOIN     -> Locate source tables and join records\n2. WHERE           -> Filter raw individual rows\n3. GROUP BY        -> Group rows into summary buckets\n4. HAVING          -> Filter aggregated group buckets\n5. SELECT          -> Compute expressions, mathematical formulas, and assign aliases\n6. DISTINCT        -> Deduplicate identical rows\n7. ORDER BY        -> Sort the final result set (Can use aliases!)\n8. LIMIT / OFFSET  -> Return the specified top N rows\n```\n\n> **Why this matters**: Because `WHERE` executes at Step 2 before `SELECT` assigns aliases at Step 5, writing `WHERE total_revenue > 100000` causes a syntax error! You must write `HAVING SUM(amount) > 100000` or filter raw columns in `WHERE`.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Summary & Best Practices\n- SQL is declarative: you describe *what* data you want, and the database query optimizer figures out *how* to fetch it.\n- Memorize the logical execution order to write error-free queries on your first attempt.",
        "challenge": {
          "type": "quiz",
          "title": "Module 1 Checkpoint: SQL Execution Order",
          "question": "Why does the query 'SELECT quantity * price AS total_amount FROM orders WHERE total_amount > 5000;' produce an error in standard SQL?",
          "options": [
            "SQL cannot multiply two columns.",
            "The WHERE clause executes before the SELECT clause assigns the 'total_amount' alias, so the database does not recognize the alias during row filtering.",
            "Aliases can only be written in uppercase letters.",
            "The LIMIT clause was omitted."
          ],
          "correctAnswer": 1,
          "explanation": "In SQL logical execution order, the WHERE clause evaluates at Step 2 to filter rows before the SELECT clause executes at Step 5 to compute aliases. Therefore, aliases created in SELECT are not visible inside WHERE."
        }
