        "summary": "Master executive communication and influence. Learn to translate technical terminology (p-values, standard deviation, CTEs) into business language (risk, ROI, customer churn), handle executive pushback, and lead decisive data meetings.",
        "content": "### Learning Objectives\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n1. Translate technical statistical jargon into actionable commercial language that resonates with CEOs, CFOs, and operations managers.\n2. Master **Executive Presence**: structured speaking, calm demeanor, maintaining eye contact, and concise delivery.\n3. Structure a 20-minute executive briefing presentation: 5 min Context & Recommendation -> 5 min Key Evidence -> 10 min Discussion & Alignment.\n4. Confidently handle skeptical pushback (\"I've been in this industry 20 years and my gut says otherwise\") using respectful, data-backed evidence.\n5. Document meeting decisions, assign action item owners, and establish tracking milestones.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Translating Technical Jargon to Executive Language\n\n```\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                       TECHNICAL vs EXECUTIVE TRANSLATION                      |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Don't Say (Technical Jargon)    | Do Say (Commercial Value / Decision)        |\n+---------------------------------+---------------------------------------------+\n| \"We ran an inner join and       | \"We identified 1,420 high-value customers   |\n| calculated the 95th percentile\" | who generate 60% of our repeat profit\"      |\n+---------------------------------+---------------------------------------------+\n| \"The regression p-value is 0.01 | \"Faster courier turnaround directly drives  |\n| with an R-squared of 0.78\"      | customer re-orders and 5-star ratings\"      |\n+---------------------------------+---------------------------------------------+\n| \"The data model has high        | \"This metric is inconsistent due to missing |\n| null cardinality in table X\"    | branch logs, which we are fixing this week\" |\n+---------------------------------+---------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Summary & Best Practices\n- Never read slides word-for-word. Use slides for visual charts and speak to the business implications.\n- Anticipate the CFO's top question: \"How much will this cost and what is the expected payback period?\"",
