        "summary": "Put theory into practice: conduct a comprehensive personal digital security audit, discover compromised accounts, master the step-by-step emergency incident response protocol, and create your personalized 10-point cyber defense plan.",
        "content": "### Learning Objectives\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n1. Conduct a full-spectrum personal cybersecurity audit across accounts, devices, and networks.\n2. Check whether your email addresses or passwords have been exposed in public data breaches.\n3. Execute the 5-step Emergency Incident Response Protocol if an account or device is compromised.\n4. Establish a personalized 10-point Digital Security Action Plan with a quarterly maintenance schedule.\n5. Practice digital resilience and maintain lifelong safe computing habits.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Personal Cybersecurity Audit\n\nJust as you conduct regular health checkups or vehicle maintenance, your digital presence requires **routine security audits**.\n\n```\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  THE 4-STAGE PERSONAL SECURITY AUDIT                     |\n|                                                                          |\n|  1. IDENTITY & CREDENTIALS | Check breached emails, weak passwords, MFA  |\n|  2. DEVICES & ENDPOINTS    | Audit OS updates, disk encryption, screen locks|\n|  3. CLOUD & APP ECOSYSTEM  | Revoke inactive third-party app permissions |\n|  4. BACKUPS & RECOVERY     | Test 3-2-1 backups & emergency recovery keys |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Checking for Breached Credentials (`HaveIBeenPwned`)\n\nWhen major online services suffer breaches, security researchers aggregate the exposed emails and hashes on public breach notification platforms like **HaveIBeenPwned.com** (created by renowned security director Troy Hunt).\n\n#### How to Conduct a Breach Check:\n1. Visit `https://haveibeenpwned.com`.\n2. Enter your primary email addresses (personal, academic, and business).\n3. Review the breach report:\n   - Identify which specific services were compromised.\n   - What data was exposed (passwords, phone numbers, birthdates).\n4. **Immediate Action:** If an exposed account used a password you still use anywhere else, change it immediately and enable MFA!\n\n---\n\n### 3. Emergency Incident Response Protocol: What to Do When Breached\n\nIf you discover unauthorized transactions, notice strange password reset emails, or lose access to an account, **do not panic—act methodically following the 5-Step Incident Response Protocol**:\n\n```\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  EMERGENCY INCIDENT RESPONSE PROTOCOL                    |\n|                                                                          |\n|  STEP 1: CONTAINMENT    | Disconnect compromised device from Wi-Fi/data. |\n|  STEP 2: PRIMARY EMAIL  | Secure your primary email FIRST (hackers use it|\n|                         | to reset all your other accounts!).            |\n|  STEP 3: REVOKE SESSIONS| Click 'Log out of all other sessions/devices'. |\n|  STEP 4: FINANCIAL ALERTS| Contact your bank, credit card company, or    |\n|                         | M-Pesa carrier to freeze accounts/cards.       |\n|  STEP 5: NOTIFY CONTACTS| Inform close contacts if social media was hacked|\n|                         | to prevent them from falling for impersonation |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. The 10-Point Personal Security Action Plan\n\nImplement these 10 actionable controls to place yourself in the **top 1% of secure digital citizens**:\n\n| Priority | Security Control Action Item | Implementation Frequency |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **01** | Set a unique 16+ character passphrase for primary email | Immediate |\n| **02** | Enable Authenticator App (TOTP) Multi-Factor Authentication | Immediate |\n| **03** | Install a trusted Password Manager (Bitwarden / 1Password) | Immediate |\n| **04** | Set a 6-digit PIN on your smartphone SIM card | Immediate |\n| **05** | Enable automatic OS and browser security updates on all devices | Immediate |\n| **06** | Configure automated 3-2-1 backup to encrypted cloud storage | Weekly |\n| **07** | Audit and log out of unrecognized active account sessions | Monthly |\n| **08** | Review third-party connected apps in Google / Microsoft settings | Quarterly |\n| **09** | Test restoring files from your backup storage | Quarterly |\n| **10** | Perform a breach check on HaveIBeenPwned for all email addresses | Semi-Annually |\n\n---\n\n### 5. Practical Templates\n\n#### Incident Response Emergency Contact & Action Sheet:\n```text\nEMERGENCY SECURITY CONTACTS:\n- Bank 24/7 Fraud Helpline: [Bank Phone Number]\n- Mobile Money Fraud Desk (M-Pesa / Telecom): [100 / 234 / Official Support]\n- Company IT Security Lead: [Internal Security Contact]\n\nBREACH CONTAINMENT LOG:\nDate & Time Detected: ____________________\nCompromised Account / Service: ____________________\nImmediate Actions Taken:\n[ ] Primary email password changed & MFA rotated\n[ ] Active sessions terminated\n[ ] Financial cards frozen / transaction limits lowered\n[ ] Support ticket filed with official provider\n```\n\n---\n\n### 6. Activities & Practical Exercises\n\n#### Exercise 1: Personal Breach Discovery\nPerform a HaveIBeenPwned search on your personal and work email addresses. Document how many past public data breaches your email was found in, and verify that you do not use old breached passwords on any current account.\n\n#### Exercise 2: Building Your 10-Point Roadmap\nDraft your personalized 10-point cybersecurity implementation roadmap using the template in Section 4. Mark the completion date for each control.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Module Knowledge Quiz\n\n**Question 1:** When responding to a suspected account takeover across multiple services, which account must you secure and change passwords on FIRST?\n- A) Your Netflix account\n- B) Your primary email account *(Correct)*\n- C) Your video game forum account\n- D) Your music playlist app\n*Explanation: Your primary email is the master recovery key for all other accounts; securing it prevents attackers from resetting passwords elsewhere.*\n\n**Question 2:** What is the primary purpose of visiting breach verification websites like HaveIBeenPwned?\n- A) To purchase hacker tools\n- B) To verify if your email address or credentials have been exposed in public corporate data breaches so you can change compromised passwords *(Correct)*\n- C) To delete your internet browsing history permanently\n- D) To test internet download speed\n*Explanation: Breach search tools inform users when their data was compromised in third-party breaches, enabling proactive credential rotation.*\n\n**Question 3:** What should you do immediately if you suspect your smartphone is infected with active spyware or malware?\n- A) Connect it to all public Wi-Fi networks in your area\n- B) Disconnect the device from internet connections (Wi-Fi and mobile data) to stop remote data exfiltration while preparing for analysis or factory reset *(Correct)*\n- C) Post your banking PIN on social media\n- D) Turn up the screen brightness to 100%\n*Explanation: Disconnecting the device stops malware from transmitting stolen credentials or receiving commands from attacker servers.*\n\n---\n\n### 8. Module Summary & Key Takeaways\n- Regularly audit your digital security posture across accounts, devices, and permissions.\n- Check HaveIBeenPwned to discover and rotate breached credentials.\n- Follow the 5-step Incident Response Protocol: Containment, Secure Primary Email, Revoke Sessions, Financial Alerts, and Notify Contacts.\n- Implement the 10-Point Personal Security Plan for lasting digital safety and resilience.",
