        "content": "\n# Module 3: Conditions (Shuroot) & Essential Pillars (Arkan) of Salah\n\nTo ensure prayer is performed validly and accepted by Allah, classical Islamic jurists systematically categorized the components of Salah into clear legal classifications based on primary textual proofs.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Defining the Legal Categories: Shart, Rukn, Wajib, and Sunnah\n\nUnderstanding these four juristic terms prevents confusion and clarifies what can and cannot be repaired if an omission occurs:\n\n1. **Condition (*Shart*, plural: *Shuroot*)**:\n   * An external prerequisite that **must be fulfilled prior to commencing prayer** and must persist unbroken throughout the entire prayer (e.g., Taharah, facing the Qiblah, covering the Awrah).\n   * *Ruling*: If a condition is violated during prayer (e.g., loss of Wudu), the prayer is immediately **invalidated** and cannot be repaired by prostrations of forgetfulness; it must be restarted from the beginning.\n2. **Pillar (*Rukn*, plural: *Arkan*)**:\n   * An essential internal component that **constitutes the actual structure of the prayer** (e.g., Takbirat al-Ihram, Ruku', Sujood).\n   * *Ruling*: If a pillar is omitted intentionally, the prayer is **invalid**. If omitted accidentally, it **cannot be compensated by Sujood as-Sahw alone**; the worshiper must physically return, perform the missed pillar, complete the prayer sequence, and then perform Sujood as-Sahw.\n3. **Obligation (*Wajib*, plural: *Wajibah*)**:\n   * A mandatory element within prayer (such as the first Tashahhud or saying *Subhana Rabbiyal 'Adheem* in Ruku' according to Hanbali and Hanafi jurisprudence).\n   * *Ruling*: If omitted intentionally, the prayer is compromised or invalid. If omitted accidentally due to forgetfulness, it **is fully repaired by performing Sujood as-Sahw** (Prostration of Forgetfulness) without having to repeat the omitted act.\n4. **Recommended Act (*Sunnah*, plural: *Sunan*)**:\n   * Voluntary actions and recitations taught by the Prophet ﷺ (such as raising hands at Takbir, looking at the place of prostration, reciting a surah after Al-Fatihah, and additional supplications).\n   * *Ruling*: Omitting a Sunnah—whether intentionally or accidentally—**does not invalidate the prayer**, and Sujood as-Sahw is not required.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Nine Conditions of Prayer (*Shuroot as-Salah*)\n\nClassical scholars enumerate **nine essential conditions** that must be satisfied before standing for prayer:\n\n1. **Islam**: Being a Muslim, as deeds are only accepted on the foundation of sound monotheistic faith.\n2. **Sanity (*Al-'Aql*)**: Being conscious, aware, and of sound mind, lifting religious obligation from the mentally incapacitated.\n3. **Discernment (*At-Tamyeez*)**: Reaching the age where a child can distinguish right from left and comprehend instructions (generally around 7 years of age).\n4. **Purification from Impurity (*Raf' al-Hadathayn*)**: Lifting minor ritual impurity through Wudu and major ritual impurity through Ghusl (or Tayammum when water is unavailable).\n5. **Removal of Physical Impurities (*Izalat an-Najasah*)**: Ensuring that physical ritual filth (*Najasah*—such as urine, blood, or feces) is removed from the worshiper's body, garments, and the exact prayer spot.\n6. **Covering the Awrah (*Satr al-'Awrah*)**:\n   * *For Men*: From the navel to the knee at minimum, with shoulders covered during prayer.\n   * *For Women*: The entire body except the face and hands (using loose, non-transparent garments that do not define shape).\n7. **Entry of the Prescribed Prayer Time (*Dukhul al-Waqt*)**: Verifying that the divinely established time window for that specific prayer has arrived; praying before its time renders the prayer invalid.\n8. **Facing the Qiblah (*Istiqbal al-Qiblah*)**: Directing one's chest and body towards the Holy Ka'bah in Makkah.\n9. **Sincere Intention (*An-Niyyah*)**: Having the conscious resolve within the heart to perform that specific prayer for the sake of Allah alone (verbal declaration is not required).\n\n---\n\n## 3. The Essential Pillars (*Arkan as-Salah*) & Classical Enumeration\n\nUnder the standard classical enumeration (prominently codified in the Shafi'i and Hanbali traditions), there are **14 foundational pillars**:\n\n1. **Standing if able (*Al-Qiyam*)** in obligatory (*Fard*) prayers.\n2. **The Opening Takbir (*Takbirat al-Ihram*)** by saying *«اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ»* (Allahu Akbar).\n3. **Reciting Surah Al-Fatihah** in every single Rak'ah.\n4. **Bowing (*Ar-Ruku'*)** until the back is level and hands grip the knees.\n5. **Straightening up from Bowing (*Al-I'tidal*)** until standing completely erect.\n6. **Prostrating on seven body parts (*As-Sujood*)** (forehead with nose, two palms, two knees, and toes of both feet).\n7. **Rising from prostration and sitting between the two prostrations (*Al-Jalsah bayna as-Sajdatayn*)**.\n8. **Tranquility and Stillness (*At-Tuma'neenah*)** in every single physical posture—pausing long enough for all joints and bones to settle calmly at rest.\n9. **The Final Tashahhud Sitting (*Al-Julus lit-Tashahhud al-Akhir*)**.\n10. **Reciting the Final Tashahhud (*At-Tashahhud al-Akhir*)**.\n11. **Sending blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ in the final sitting (*As-Salah 'ala an-Nabi ﷺ*)**.\n12. **Concluding with the Tasleem (*At-Tasleem*)** by saying *«السَّلاَمُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ»*.\n13. **Observing the proper sequential order (*At-Tarteeb*)** between all pillars.\n14. **Calm continuity and composure throughout the prayer**.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Understanding Juristic Classifications Across the Four Madhahib\n\nWhile all four Sunni schools agree upon the core rituals taught by the Prophet ﷺ, their juristic terminology, counts, and classifications vary based on their methodological interpretation of authentic texts:\n\n* **The Hanafi School**: Distinguishes strictly between *Fard* (decisively proven obligation) and *Wajib* (probabilistically proven obligation). The internal *Fara'id* (pillars) of prayer are fewer: Takbirat al-Ihram (as an entering condition/pillar), Qiyam, Qira'ah (reciting any portion of the Qur'an), Ruku', Sujood, and the final sitting (*Qa'dah Akhirah*) for the duration of the Tashahhud. Reciting Surah Al-Fatihah specifically, reciting an additional Surah, the first sitting, and Tuma'neenah (in the dominant view) are categorized as *Wajib*.\n* **The Maliki School**: Counts *Niyyah* (intention) as an integral pillar inside the prayer itself rather than a prior condition. Pillars include Takbirat al-Ihram, Qiyam for it, Fatihah in every Rak'ah, Qiyam for Fatihah, Ruku', rising from Ruku', Sujood, rising from Sujood, sitting for the Tasleem, the single Tasleem, Tuma'neenah, and Tarteeb.\n* **The Shafi'i School**: Counts 13 or 17 pillars, notably classifying Intention (*Niyyah*) as an internal pillar, treating *«بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ»* as an obligatory verse of Surah Al-Fatihah without which Fatihah is incomplete, counting Tuma'neenah in each posture as a distinct pillar, and including Salawat on the Prophet ﷺ in the final sitting.\n* **The Hanbali School**: Fully enumerates the 14 Arkan and clearly distinguishes them from the 8 *Wajibah* (such as the Takbirs of transition, saying *Subhana Rabbiyal 'Adheem* in Ruku', saying *Subhana Rabbiyal A'la* in Sujood, saying *Rabbighfir lee* between prostrations, and the first Tashahhud).\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> None of these classical juristic differences imply that one school is \"right\" and another is \"wrong.\" Each school's framework represents centuries of rigorous scholarly devotion to implementing the authentic Sunnah of Prophet Muhammad ﷺ.\n      ",
