        "content": "\n# Module 6: Common Mistakes, Sujood as-Sahw & Special Prayers\n\nIn this module, we examine practical scenarios: rectifying prayer errors, participating in congregational prayers, and understanding special prayer formats.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Common Physical & Procedural Mistakes\n\n### 1. Rushing Without Stillness (*'Adam at-Tuma'neenah*)\n* **The Error**: Moving rapidly between postures like a pecking crow without pausing.\n* **The Classical Rule**: The worshiper is not required to count an arbitrary number of seconds (such as a modern claim of \"exactly 3 seconds\"). Rather, **every single joint, bone, and limb must fully settle into resting position** before transitioning to the next posture. Classical jurists define the minimum necessary stillness as the time required to recite the obligatory tasbih once with calm composure (*Subhana Rabbiyal 'Adheem* or *Subhana Rabbiyal A'la*). Rushing without this stillness invalidates the pillar.\n\n### 2. Looking Around or Gazing Upward\n* **The Error**: Looking up at the ceiling or glancing sideways during prayer.\n* **The Ruling**: The Prophet ﷺ issued a stern warning:\n  > *«لَيَنْتَهِيَنَّ أَقْوَامٌ يَرْفَعُونَ أَبْصَارَهُمْ إِلَى السَّمَاءِ فِي الصَّلاَةِ أَوْ لاَ تَرْجِعُ إِلَيْهِمْ»*  \n  > *\"People must desist from looking up to the sky during prayer, or else their sight will not return to them.\"* (Sahih Muslim 428).\n* **The Sunnah**: Keep your gaze fixed steadily on the spot of prostration while standing, bowing, and prostrating, and on the index finger during Tashahhud.\n\n### 3. Laying Forearms Flat on the Ground in Sujood\n* **The Error**: Resting the elbows and forearms on the floor like a resting animal.\n* **The Ruling**: The Prophet ﷺ commanded:\n  > *«اعْتَدِلُوا فِي السُّجُودِ، وَلاَ يَبْسُطْ أَحَدُكُمْ ذِرَاعَيْهِ انْبِسَاطَ الْكَلْبِ»*  \n  > *\"Be moderate in prostration and let not one of you lay his forearms flat [on the floor] like a dog.\"* (Sahih al-Bukhari 822).\n* **The Sunnah**: Elevate the elbows and forearms completely off the floor, resting only on the palms.\n\n### 4. Preceding or Racing the Imam (*Musabaqat al-Imam*)\n* **The Error**: Moving into Ruku' or Sujood before or simultaneously with the Imam.\n* **The Ruling**: The Prophet ﷺ explicitly commanded:\n  > *«إِنَّمَا جُعِلَ الإِمَامُ لِيُؤْتَمَّ بِهِ، فَإِذَا كَبَّرَ فَكَبِّرُوا، وَإِذَا رَكَعَ فَارْكَعُوا، وَإِذَا قَالَ سَمِعَ اللَّهُ لِمَنْ حَمِدَهُ فَقُولُوا رَبَّنَا وَلَكَ الْحَمْدُ، وَإِذَا سَجَدَ فَاسْجُدُوا...»*  \n  > *\"The Imam is appointed only to be followed: when he says Takbir, then say Takbir; when he bows, then bow; when he says 'Sami'a Allahu liman hamidah', then say 'Rabbana wa lakal-hamd'; and when he prostrates, then prostrate...\"* (Sahih al-Bukhari 722, Sahih Muslim 414).\n* **The Sunnah**: Begin your movement only **after** the Imam finishes uttering the transition Takbir, never anticipating or competing.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Prostration of Forgetfulness (*Sujood as-Sahw*)\n\nHuman forgetfulness in prayer is natural. Islam provides a standardized, merciful remedy called **Sujood as-Sahw** (two prostrations performed at the end of prayer) for three specific causes:\n\n1. **Addition (*Az-Ziyadah*)**: Accidentally adding an extra Rak'ah, Ruku', or Sujood.\n2. **Omission (*An-Naqs*)**: Accidentally omitting an obligatory act (*Wajib*), such as forgetting the first Tashahhud and standing up.\n3. **Doubt (*Ash-Shakk*)**: Experiencing uncertainty (e.g., whether you prayed 3 or 4 Rak'ahs).\n\n### Recognized Procedures Across the Four Madhahib:\n* **The Shafi'i School**: The two prostrations of forgetfulness are **always performed before the Tasleem** in every scenario, immediately after finishing the Tashahhud and Salawat.\n* **The Hanafi School**: The two prostrations are **always performed after a single Tasleem to the right**. The worshiper makes one Tasleem to the right, performs the two Sujoods, sits again, recites the Tashahhud and Salawat, and concludes with the standard two Tasleems.\n* **The Maliki School**: Distinguishes between addition and omission:\n  * For an **Omission (*Naqs*)** (or combined omission and addition): Perform the two Sujoods **before the Tasleem**.\n  * For a pure **Addition (*Ziyadah*)**: Perform the two Sujoods **after the Tasleem**.\n* **The Hanbali School**:\n  * Perform **before the Tasleem** for omissions, and when in doubt building upon certainty (the lower number).\n  * Perform **after the Tasleem** for additions, and when the worshiper builds upon a predominant assumption (*Ghalabat adh-Dhann*).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Special & Congregational Prayers\n\n### Friday Prayer (*Salat al-Jumu'ah*)\n* An individual obligation (*Fard 'Ayn*) upon adult, resident Muslim men in place of Dhuhr prayer.\n* Preceded by two **Khutbahs** (sermons delivered by the Khatib).\n* *Etiquette*: The listener must remain completely silent and attentive. The Prophet ﷺ taught that even whispering *\"listen attentively\"* to a companion while the Imam is preaching negates the reward of Jumu'ah (Sahih al-Bukhari 934).\n* Consists of **2 Rak'ahs recited aloud** behind the Imam.\n\n### Funeral Prayer (*Salat al-Janazah*)\n* A communal obligation (*Fard Kifayah*) performed **entirely standing with no Ruku' or Sujood**:\n  * **1st Takbir**: Seek refuge and recite Surah Al-Fatihah quietly.\n  * **2nd Takbir**: Recite Salawat Ibrahimiyyah upon the Prophet ﷺ.\n  * **3rd Takbir**: Make sincere supplication for the deceased (e.g., *«اللَّهُمَّ اغْفِرْ لَهُ وَارْحَمْهُ وَعَافِهِ وَاعْفُ عَنْهُ...»*).\n  * **4th Takbir**: Pause briefly with supplication for the Muslim community, then conclude with Tasleem to the right.\n\n### The Traveler's Concession (*Salat al-Musafir*)\n* A divine concession (*Rukhsah*): When embarking upon a qualifying journey (approximately 80 km or more according to classical jurists):\n  * **Shortening (*Qasr*)**: The 4-Rak'ah prayers (Dhuhr, Asr, Isha) are shortened to **2 Rak'ahs** each. Fajr and Maghrib are never shortened.\n  * **Combining (*Jam'*)**: Dhuhr may be combined with Asr, and Maghrib may be combined with Isha, either by bringing the second prayer forward (*Jam' Taqdeem*) or delaying the first (*Jam' Ta'kheer*).\n      ",
