        "content": "# Chapter 1: English Alphabet, Phonetics & Essential Sounds\n\nWelcome to **English Language Learning** on FAHMRISE. The journey to confident English fluency begins with clear pronunciation and phonetic awareness.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The 26 Letters vs. The 44 Sounds\n\nEnglish writing uses a 26-letter Latin alphabet, but spoken English contains **44 phonemes**:\n- **20 Vowel Sounds**: 12 pure monophthongs (short and long) + 8 diphthongs (gliding vowels like /aɪ/ in *my* and /eɪ/ in *day*).\n- **24 Consonant Sounds**: Voiced sounds (vocal cords vibrate) and unvoiced sounds (vocal cords do not vibrate).\n\n| Letter Group | Example Sounds | Example Words |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Short Vowels** | /ɪ/, /e/, /æ/, /ʌ/, /ɒ/, /ʊ/ | *bit, bed, bat, but, box, book* |\n| **Long Vowels** | /iː/, /ɑː/, /ɔː/, /uː/, /ɜː/ | *beat, calm, thought, boot, bird* |\n| **Diphthongs** | /eɪ/, /aɪ/, /ɔɪ/, /aʊ/, /əʊ/ | *say, fly, boy, now, go* |\n| **Consonants** | /p/ vs /b/, /t/ vs /d/, /k/ vs /g/ | *pat/bat, ten/den, cut/gut* |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Voiced vs. Unvoiced Consonants\n\nPlace your fingers gently on your throat (adam's apple):\n- When saying **/b/**, **/d/**, **/g/**, **/v/**, or **/z/**, you will feel a vibration. These are **voiced**.\n- When saying **/p/**, **/t/**, **/k/**, **/f/**, or **/s/**, there is only a puff of air with no vibration. These are **unvoiced**.\n\n### The Crucial 'TH' Sounds\n1. **Unvoiced /θ/**: *Think, Thank, Three, Birthday, Month*.\n2. **Voiced /ð/**: *This, That, Mother, Together, Breathe*.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Syllable Stress & The Schwa (/ə/) Sound\n\nIn English, multi-syllable words always have one syllable that receives the primary stress. Saying the stress in the wrong position can make words difficult for native speakers to recognize.\n\n> **Rule of Thumb:**\n> - Most two-syllable **nouns** stress the first syllable: **TA-ble**, **DOC-tor**, **WA-ter**.\n> - Most two-syllable **verbs** stress the second syllable: **de-CIDE**, **be-GIN**, **re-LAX**.\n\nThe **Schwa (/ə/)** is the unstressed central vowel. It appears in words like:\n- **a-BOUT** (/əˈbaʊt/)\n- **ba-NA-na** (/bəˈnɑː.nə/)\n- **COM-pu-ter** (/kəmˈpjuː.tə/)",
