        "content": "# Chapter 5: Listening Comprehension & Auditory Skills\n\nBridging the gap between reading English and understanding spoken English.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why Spoken English Sounds Fast: Connected Speech\n\nWhen native speakers talk, words are not pronounced as separate blocks. They flow together:\n\n1. **Consonant to Vowel Linking:**\n   - *An apple* ➔ Sounds like *\"a-napple\"*\n   - *Hold on* ➔ Sounds like *\"hol-don\"*\n2. **Weak Forms:**\n   - *to* becomes /tə/ (*\"I want to go\"* ➔ *\"I wanna go\"*)\n   - *for* becomes /fə/ (*\"This is for you\"* ➔ *\"This is f'you\"*)\n3. **Sound Merging:**\n   - *Could you* ➔ /kʊdʒuː/\n   - *Would you* ➔ /wʊdʒuː/\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Ear-Training Strategy\n- **Step 1:** Listen to a 1-minute audio clip without subtitles. Write down the main topic.\n- **Step 2:** Listen a second time and write down the key content words.\n- **Step 3:** Check against the transcript to observe which sounds were reduced or linked.",
