        "content": "# Chapter 1: French Alphabet, Phonetics & Accents\n\nBienvenue au cours de français sur FAHMRISE! French is a melodious, syllable-timed Romance language renowned for its expressive cadence, nasal vowels, and nuanced orthography.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The French Alphabet & Distinctive Letters\n\n| Letter | French Name | IPA | Articulation & Phonetic Notes | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **A** | /ɑ/ | /a/ ~ /ɑ/ | Open unrounded front or back vowel | *ami* (/a.mi/), *table* (/tabl/) |\n| **E** | /ə/ | /ə/ | Soft central schwa sound (*le*, *je*); silent at the end of multi-syllable words (*e caduc*) | *petit* (/pə.ti/), *monde* (/mɔ̃d/) |\n| **G** | /ʒe/ | /ɡ/ or /ʒ/ | Hard /ɡ/ before **a, o, u**; soft /ʒ/ (like 's' in *pleasure*) before **e, i, y** | *gare* (/ɡaʁ/), *gîte* (/ʒit/) |\n| **H** | /aʃ/ | [silent] | **Never pronounced as a breath**. Divided into *H muet* and *H aspiré* (see Section 3) | *homme* (/ɔm/), *héros* (/e.ʁo/) |\n| **J** | /ʒi/ | /ʒ/ | Always voiced postalveolar fricative /ʒ/ (never English /dʒ/) | *jour* (/ʒuʁ/), *je* (/ʒə/) |\n| **R** | /ɛʁ/ | /ʁ/ | Voiced uvular fricative produced at the back of the throat near the uvula | *rouge* (/ʁuʒ/), *merci* (/mɛʁ.si/) |\n| **U** | /y/ | /y/ | Close front rounded vowel: round lips for /u/ ('oo') while shaping tongue for /i/ ('ee'). Contrasts with *ou* (/u/) | *tu* (/ty/) vs *tout* (/tu/), *lune* (/lyn/) |\n\n---\n\n## 2. The 5 French Accent Marks: Precise Linguistic Functions\n\nFrench orthography employs five accent marks with distinct phonological and morphological roles:\n\n1. **L'Accent Aigu (é)**:\n   - Appears **only on the letter 'E'**.\n   - Produces a closed front unrounded vowel: **/e/** (as in *café*, *été*, *étudiant*, *vérité*).\n\n2. **L'Accent Grave (è, à, ù)**:\n   - **On è**: Produces an open front unrounded vowel: **/ɛ/** (as in *père*, *mère*, *très*, *pièce*).\n   - **On à and ù**: Crucially, **does NOT change vowel pronunciation**. Its sole function is to distinguish semantic and grammatical **homophones**:\n     - *a* (verb *avoir*: \"he/she has\") vs. *à* (preposition: \"to / at / in\").\n     - *ou* (conjunction: \"or\") vs. *où* (interrogative/relative adverb: \"where\").\n     - *la* (feminine article: \"the\") vs. *là* (locative adverb: \"there\").\n\n3. **L'Accent Circonflexe (â, ê, î, ô, û)**:\n   - Historically marks the **elision of an ancient consonant** (most commonly a historical 's' that disappeared from Old French):\n     - *hôpital* (Old French *hospital*)\n     - *forêt* (Old French *forest*)\n     - *château* (Latin *castellum*, Old French *chastel*)\n     - *fête* (Old French *feste*)\n   - Can also indicate vowel lengthening or distinct vowel quality (e.g., *pâte* /pɑt/ [dough] vs. *patte* /pat/ [paw]).\n\n4. **La Cédille (ç)**:\n   - Placed exclusively beneath the letter **'C' before back vowels (a, o, u)** to maintain a soft **/s/** sound instead of a hard /k/:\n     - *français* (/fʁɑ̃.sɛ/), *garçon* (/ɡaʁ.sɔ̃/), *leçon* (/lə.sɔ̃/), *ça* (/sa/).\n   - **Rule**: A cédille is never placed before front vowels 'e' or 'i', where 'c' is naturally soft (*merci*, *ceci*, *cinéma*).\n\n5. **Le Tréma (ë, ï, ü)**:\n   - Placed above the second of two adjacent vowels to indicate **diaeresis** (that both vowels must be pronounced independently rather than merging into a diphthong or digraph):\n     - *Noël* (/nɔ.ɛl/) — pronounced in two syllables, not a single sound.\n     - *maïs* (/ma.is/ = sweetcorn) vs. *mais* (/mɛ/ = but).\n     - *naïf* (/na.if/) and *coïncidence* (/kɔ.ɛ̃.si.dɑ̃s/).\n\n---\n\n## 3. The Letter H: \"H Muet\" vs. \"H Aspiré\"\n\nWhile the letter **H** is never articulated as an audible consonant in standard French, it strictly governs two essential phonological phenomena: **Elision** (*l'élision*) and **Liaison** (*la liaison*).\n\n| Feature | H Muet (Mute H) | H Aspiré (Aspirated H) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Grammatical Status** | Behaves like a vowel | Behaves like a consonant |\n| **Elision (dropping vowel)** | **Permitted**: *l'homme*, *l'hôtel*, *l'heure* | **Forbidden**: *le héros* (never *l'héros*), *la hache* (never *l'hache*), *le homard* |\n| **Liaison (linking 's' as /z/)** | **Mandatory**: *les‿hommes* (/le.z‿ɔm/), *les‿hôtels* (/le.z‿o.tɛl/) | **Strictly Forbidden**: *les héros* (/le e.ʁo/, never */le.z‿e.ʁo/*) |\n| **Etymological Origin** | Sourced from Latin / Greco-Latin roots | Sourced from Germanic or foreign loanwords |\n\n---\n\n## 4. The 4 French Nasal Vowels\n\nNasal vowels occur when air resonates simultaneously through both the mouth and nose without sounding a separate final 'n' or 'm':\n\n1. **/ɑ̃/**: Written *an, am, en, em* (e.g., *vent* /vɑ̃/, *enfant* /ɑ̃.fɑ̃/, *chambre* /ʃɑ̃bʁ/, *France* /fʁɑ̃s/).\n2. **/ɛ̃/**: Written *in, im, ain, aim, ein* (e.g., *vin* /vɛ̃/, *timbre* /tɛ̃bʁ/, *pain* /pɛ̃/, *faim* /fɛ̃/, *plein* /plɛ̃/).\n3. **/ɔ̃/**: Written *on, om* (e.g., *bon* /bɔ̃/, *nom* /nɔ̃/, *maison* /mɛ.zɔ̃/, *monde* /mɔ̃d/).\n4. **/œ̃/**: Written *un, um* (e.g., *un* /œ̃/, *lundi* /lœ̃.di/, *parfum* /paʁ.fœ̃/).",
