        "transcript": "Karibu! Welcome to Chapter 1 of Swahili Language Learning. Kiswahili is a phonetic Bantu language, which means words are pronounced consistently as written. Every vowel is pure: A (/a/), E (/e/ as in 'meza'), I (/i/), O (/o/), U (/u/). Crucially, when 'm' or 'n' precedes another consonant at the start of a word (like 'm-tu' or 'm-ji'), it is syllabic and forms its own syllable. The universal stress rule in Kiswahili places stress on the penultimate (second-to-last) syllable.",
