        "content": "# Chapter 1: Kiswahili Alphabet, Sounds & Syllable Stress\n\nKaribu sana! Welcome to **Swahili Language Learning** on FAHMRISE. Kiswahili is a premier Bantu lingua franca spoken by over 200 million people across Eastern, Central, and Southern Africa. It holds official language status in **Kenya, Tanzania, Uganda, and Rwanda**, as well as the **East African Community (EAC)**, the **African Union (AU)**, and the **Southern African Development Community (SADC)**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The 5 Pure Vowels of Kiswahili\n\nUnlike English vowels which frequently diphthongize (glide), Kiswahili vowels are always **pure, constant, and unglided**:\n\n| Vowel | IPA Sound | English Approximation | Kiswahili Example | Meaning |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **A** | /a/ | Like *a* in *father* | **Baba** (/ba.ba/) | Father |\n| **E** | /e/ | Like *e* in *bed* (open-mid front) | **Meza** (/me.za/) or **Ndege** (/ⁿde.ge/) | Table / Bird |\n| **I** | /i/ | Like *ee* in *see* (close front) | **Mji** (/m.ji/) | Town / City |\n| **O** | /o/ | Like *o* in *for* (close-mid back) | **Moyo** (/mo.yo/) | Heart |\n| **U** | /u/ | Like *oo* in *food* (close back) | **Mtu** (/m.tu/) | Person |\n\n> 💡 *Note on Vowel 'E':* Common learner mistakes involve selecting words like *mwalimu* as 'E' examples; *mwalimu* contains only vowels **a**, **i**, and **u**. Genuine examples of 'E' include **meza** (table), **ndege** (bird/aeroplane), **wewe** (you), and **yeye** (he/she).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Syllable Structure & The Syllabic 'M' and 'N'\n\nKiswahili syllable structure is typically open (consonant + vowel: **CV**), meaning syllables almost always terminate in a vowel:\n- *Ki-ta-bu* (book) ➔ 3 open syllables: /ki.ta.bu/\n- *Ba-ba* (father) ➔ 2 open syllables: /ba.ba/\n\n### Syllabic Consonants:\nWhen the prefix **m-** or **n-** precedes another consonant at the start of a noun (common in noun classes 1, 2, 9, 10), it is **syllabic**—it forms its own distinct vocal beat/syllable without any vowel:\n- **M-tu** (person) ➔ 2 syllables: /ˈm.tu/\n- **M-ji** (city / town) ➔ 2 syllables: /ˈm.ji/\n- **M-bwa** (dog) ➔ 2 syllables: /ˈm.bwa/\n- **N-chi** (country / nation) ➔ 2 syllables: /ˈn.tʃi/\n\n---\n\n## 3. The Golden Stress Rule: Penultimate Syllable\n\nIn standard Kiswahili, word-level stress is exceptionally regular and predictable: **it almost universally falls on the penultimate (second-to-last) syllable**:\n\n- **Ki-swa-HI-li** (Swahili)\n- **ha-BA-ri** (news)\n- **a-SAN-te** (thank you)\n- **ta-FA-dha-li** (please)\n- **M-tu** (/ˈm.tu/) ➔ Because 'm' is syllabic, it is the second-to-last syllable and takes the stress!",
