        "content": "# Chapter 3: Swahili Greetings (Habari, Hujambo, Shikamoo) & Etiquette\n\nIn East Africa, greetings are not a mere formality—they establish rapport, respect, and community connection.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The 4 Essential Greeting Categories\n\n### A. The General State Greeting (Jambo)\n- **Question (Singular):** *Hujambo?* (Any problems/state with you?)\n- **Answer (Singular):** *Sijambo!* (I have no problems / I am fine!)\n- **Question (Plural):** *Hamjambo?* (How are you all?)\n- **Answer (Plural):** *Hatujambo!* (We are all fine!)\n\n### B. The News Greeting (Habari)\n- *Habari za asubuhi?* = Good morning (lit. news of the morning)\n- *Habari za mchana?* = Good afternoon\n- *Habari za jioni?* = Good evening\n- *Habari za kazi?* = How is work?\n- **Answers:** *Nzuri* (good), *Salama* (peaceful), *Safi* (great).\n\n### C. The Elder Respect Greeting (Shikamoo)\n- **Youth to Elder:** *Shikamoo!*\n- **Elder's Response:** *Marahaba!*",
