        "transcript": "Hiragana is the foundation of all Japanese literacy. The 46 basic characters are structured in the Gojūon (50-sound) grid across vowel columns (A, I, U, E, O) and consonant rows (K, S, T, N, H, M, Y, R, W, and singular N). Adding Dakuten (two dots ゛) voices consonants: K becomes G (が ぎ ぐ げ ご), S becomes Z (ざ じ ず ぜ ぞ), T becomes D (だ ぢ づ で ど), and H becomes B (ば び ぶ べ ぼ). Adding Handakuten (a small circle ゜) turns H into P (ぱ ぴ ぷ ぺ ぽ). Combining I-row characters with small ya, yu, yo creates contracted glide sounds (Yōon) such as kya, kyu, kyo, sha, shu, sho.",
