        "transcript": "Kanji are ideographic characters where each character embodies meaning. Most Kanji possess two types of readings: Onyomi (音読み, Chinese-derived pronunciation, usually used in compound words) and Kunyomi (訓読み, native Japanese pronunciation, usually used when the character stands alone or with Hiragana endings). Mastering the core building blocks—radicals (bushu)—allows you to decipher complex Kanji with ease. Foundational Kanji include numbers (一, 二, 三, 十, 百), nature (日 sun/day, 月 moon/month, 木 tree, 水 water, 火 fire, 山 mountain, 川 river), and people (人 person, 男 man, 女 woman).",
