        "content": "# Chapter 4: Basic Kanji & Recognition\n\nKanji (漢字) gives Japanese its depth and visual clarity. While there are thousands of characters, beginner fluency requires recognizing foundational pictographs.\n\n---\n\n## Onyomi vs. Kunyomi\n\n| Kanji | Meaning | Kunyomi (Native Japanese) | Onyomi (Sino-Japanese) | Example Word |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **日** | Sun, Day | *hi, bi* | *nichi, jitsu* | **日本** (*Nihon* - Japan) |\n| **月** | Moon, Month | *tsuki* | *getsu, gatsu* | **今月** (*kongetsu* - this month) |\n| **木** | Tree, Wood | *ki* | *moku, boku* | **木曜日** (*mokuyōbi* - Thursday) |\n| **水** | Water | *mizu* | *sui* | **水泳** (*suiei* - swimming) |\n| **火** | Fire | *hi* | *ka* | **火山** (*kazan* - volcano) |\n| **山** | Mountain | *yama* | *san* | **富士山** (*Fujisan* - Mt. Fuji) |\n| **人** | Person | *hito* | *jin, nin* | **日本人** (*Nihonjin* - Japanese person) |\n\n---\n\n## Basic Numbers in Kanji\n\n- **一** (ichi) = 1\n- **二** (ni) = 2\n- **三** (san) = 3\n- **四** (yon/shi) = 4\n- **五** (go) = 5\n- **六** (roku) = 6\n- **七** (nana/shichi) = 7\n- **八** (hachi) = 8\n- **九** (kyuu/ku) = 9\n- **十** (juu) = 10\n- **百** (hyaku) = 100\n- **千** (sen) = 1,000\n- **万** (man) = 10,000",
