        "transcript": "The Somali alphabet contains 21 consonants and 5 vowels. Consonants include: B, T, J, X, KH, D, R, S, SH, DH, C, G, F, Q, K, L, M, N, W, H, Y. Pay special attention to the unique sounds: X represents a deep, breathy pharyngeal 'H' (as in 'Xasan' or 'Xaraf'); C represents a deep voiced pharyngeal squeeze from the throat (as in 'Cali' or 'Cunto'); Q is a deep guttural 'K' produced at the uvula (as in 'Qalin' or 'Quraac'); DH is a retroflex sound produced by curling the tip of the tongue back against the hard palate (as in 'Dhadhan' or 'Dhalinyaro'); KH is a raspy friction sound (as in 'Khad' or 'Khamiis'). Somali vowels come in two distinct lengths: short (A, E, I, O, U) and long (AA, EE, II, OO, UU). Long vowels are held for double duration.",
